Let's find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$. 

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{\left( \dfrac{dy}{d\theta} \right)}{\left( \dfrac{dx}{d\theta} \right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\sin(4\theta)\sin(\theta)+\sin^2(2\theta)\cos(\theta)}{2\sin(4\theta)\cos(\theta)-\sin^2(2\theta)\sin(\theta)}
\end{align}$

[[☃ explanation 1]]

**Note:** Because $\sin(2\theta)\neq0$ when $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{4}$, we can divide throughout by $\sin(2\theta)$ to simplify.

Evaluating $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ at $\blueD{\theta = \dfrac{\pi}{4}}$ gives us the slope of our tangent line.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\greenD{\left. \dfrac{dy}{dx}\right| _{\theta=\tfrac{\pi}{3}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\sin\left(4\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)\right)\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)+\sin^2\left(2\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)\right)\cos\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)}{2\sin\left(4\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)\right)\cos\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)-\sin^2\left(2\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)\right)\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\sin(\pi)\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)+\sin^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)}{2\sin(\pi)\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)-\sin^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2(0)\left(\dfrac{\sqrt 2}{2}\right)+(1)^2\left(\dfrac{\sqrt 2}{2}\right)}{2(0)\left(\dfrac{\sqrt 2}{2}\right)-(1)^2\left(\dfrac{\sqrt 2}{2}\right)}
\\\\
&=\greenD{-1}
\end{align}$
